{#
  The whole point of this page is that it is *one* target. Per-route full-page
  snapshots across two themes and three viewports is forty-plus baselines that
  nobody maintains; when they start failing for unrelated reasons the suite gets
  switched off. Everything renderable appears here once, so a token change shows
  up as one diff on one page.

  Two rules for editing it:
    1. Use the real component markup, copied from the page that owns it. A
       styleguide that invents its own classes tests nothing.
    2. Numbers come from `{% metric %}` like anywhere else. Typing "351" here to
       "just show the component" is exactly the drift the data layer prevents.
#}

<h2>Typography</h2>

<p>Body copy sets the measure. This paragraph exists to show the line length, the
leading and the <a href="{{ '/work/' | url }}">inline link</a> treatment, along with
<strong>bold</strong>, <em>italic</em>, <code>inline code</code> and an
abbreviation such as <abbr title="Large Language Model">LLM</abbr>.</p>

<h3>Third-level heading</h3>

<p>A second paragraph, so the vertical rhythm between a heading and the text under
it is visible rather than inferred.</p>

<blockquote>
    <p>A pull quote, used where a case study needs to sit a claim apart from the
    narrative around it.</p>
</blockquote>

<ul>
    <li>An unordered list item</li>
    <li>A second item, long enough to wrap onto a second line at the narrow
    viewport so the hanging indent is covered</li>
</ul>

<ol>
    <li>An ordered list item</li>
    <li>A second ordered item</li>
</ol>

<h2>Metrics</h2>

<p>Four confidence levels, four different renderings. The hedge is added by the
shortcode, never typed, which is what stops it drifting from the evidence.</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Exact</strong> renders bare:
    {% metric "golden_dataset_india_mh" %} golden-dataset cases.</li>

    <li><strong>Approximate</strong> prefixes "roughly":
    {% metric "regression_suite_runtime" %} for the full CI regression suite.</li>

    <li><strong>Estimate</strong> prefixes "approximately" and shows its basis:
    {% metric "anomaly_sampling_yield" %}</li>

    <li><strong>Projection</strong> prefixes "an estimated" and carries a visible
    footnote it cannot be rendered without: {% metric "pact_blast_radius" %}</li>
</ul>

<h2>Architecture note</h2>

<p>One phrasing, everywhere, because paraphrasing it is how it becomes wrong.</p>

{% architectureNote "short" %}

<h2>Charts and diagrams</h2>

<p>Hand-authored SVG, drawn from the site's own published data or a fixed
architecture description — never a stock illustration. <code>{% raw %}{% chart %}{% endraw %}</code>
throws if its metric is missing or unpublished, so a chart cannot outrun the data layer.</p>

{% chart "golden_dataset_growth" %}

{% diagram "test-pyramid" %}

