        <p class="definition-list__description">Its one-sentence description.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<h2>Cards</h2>

<p>The whole card is clickable: the title's own link is the only link inside
it, extended to the card's bounds. There is nothing here for a screen reader
to disambiguate, and nothing here breaks if a card ever gains a second link.</p>

<ul class="card-list">
    <li class="card card--case-study">
        <p class="card__eyebrow">2025 – 2026</p>
        <h3 class="card__title"><a href="{{ '/work/' | url }}">Case study card</a></h3>
        <p class="card__outcome">The one-line, number-free answer for a reader who is scanning rather than reading.</p>
        <p class="card__body">The card used on the work index, with an eyebrow, a
        linked title and a body.</p>
    </li>
    <li class="card card--case-study card--flagship">
        <p class="card__eyebrow">2025 – 2026 <span class="card__flag">Start here</span></p>
        <h3 class="card__title"><a href="{{ '/work/' | url }}">Flagship case study card</a></h3>
        <p class="card__body">The one entry a reader hiring for this specific
        work should read first. The flag is a real signal, not decoration.</p>
    </li>
    <li class="card card--post">
        <p class="card__eyebrow"><time datetime="2026-01-01">1 January 2026</time></p>
        <h3 class="card__title"><a href="{{ '/writing/' | url }}">Post card</a></h3>
        <p class="card__body">The card used on the writing index.</p>
    </li>
</ul>

<div class="card card--plain">
    <h3 class="card__title">Plain card</h3>
    <p class="card__body">Used for the open-source list and the "what I own"
    section.</p>
    <p class="card__meta">Python · MIT · {% metric "golden_dataset_hpv" %} cases</p>
</div>

